\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Reflection positivity without a quotient time shift}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On time sites #{\mathbb Z/6}, let #{\phi_t} be independent
centered real Gaussians with variances
##{(v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4,v_5)=(1,1,2,3,3,2).}
Use the link reflection #{\theta(t)=1-t} and positive half
#{X_+=\{1,2,3\}}. Since #{v_{\theta t}=v_t}, the product law
is reflection invariant. The halves are independent and their
laws agree under reflection, so for every positive-half
square-integrable #{F},
##{B_\mu(F,F)=|\mathbb E_\mu F|^2\ge0.}
This proves reflection positivity directly, without a
translation-invariance assumption.}

\p{Define a one-step shift of a coordinate expression by replacing
each #{\phi_t} by #{\phi_{t+1}}. On expressions whose sites
remain in #{X_+}, this at least gives another positive-half
function. In particular,
##{F=\phi_1^2-1,\qquad \tau F=\phi_2^2-1}
both belong to the positive-time #{L^2} domain. Their pairings are
##{B_\mu(F,F)=0,\qquad B_\mu(\tau F,\tau F)=1.}
Indeed their expectations are #{v_1-1=0} and #{v_2-1=1}.
The centering constant is part of the shifted expression and
does not change from #{1} to #{v_2}.}

\p{Thus a zero vector in the
[reflection quotient](fdip-007M) would be sent to a nonzero one.
The shift cannot descend even on this pair of positive-half
polynomials. A transfer operator requires additional structure;
reflection positivity alone does not construct it.}
